Title: Six A.M.
Credit: original short drama; names, events and dialogue are new
Author: Claude (draft)
Format: 7 scenes, 17 Flow generations of 4-8 s (one shot each), about 100 s, English dialogue
Tests: P19-01 scale run of the hardened Flow sender; two people seated in one booth for the whole film, one envelope placed before the film starts and never handled

INT. KELLER'S DINER - NIGHT

[[look: small diner after closing at night, one lit booth by a rain-streaked window, red vinyl booth seats with high backs, a warm lamp over the booth table, a red glow from outside, a thick white envelope on the table]]

RUTH KELLER, sixties, owner of the diner, sits in the booth in her apron, a coffee mug in front of her. Across the table sits SAM DOYLE, twenty-one, in a wet grey hoodie. A thick white envelope lies on the table between them.

RUTH
We closed an hour ago.

SAM
I know. I waited till the last customer left.

INT. KELLER'S DINER - NIGHT

SAM
Ten years ago, somebody took money from your till.

RUTH
Three hundred and forty dollars. On a Tuesday.

SAM
It's in the envelope. All of it, and some more.

The envelope lies on the table between them. Nobody touches it.

INT. KELLER'S DINER - NIGHT

RUTH
Why now?

SAM
I'm twenty-one. I want to start my life owing no one.

INT. KELLER'S DINER - NIGHT

RUTH
I knew it was you. Your red bike was out front.

SAM stares at her. He did not know she knew.

RUTH
You were eleven. You came back for pie every Sunday after.

INT. KELLER'S DINER - NIGHT

SAM looks down at the envelope.

SAM
Please. Take it.

RUTH looks at the envelope for a long moment.

RUTH
My cook quits on Friday.

INT. KELLER'S DINER - NIGHT

SAM
You'd hire me?

RUTH
I'd hire somebody who comes back.

INT. KELLER'S DINER - NIGHT

RUTH
Keep the money. Buy shoes that don't slip. Six a.m.

SAM smiles and nods. The envelope stays where it is. Rain runs down the window.
